+**Edge Cases:**
+*   **Overfitting:** Since the data is sparse, excessive fine-tuning may overfit to noise or specific viewpoints [S1].
+*   **Degenerate Input:** If the unseen subject has extreme poses not seen during pre-training, the deformation field may fail to generalize even after fine-tuning [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Faster than training a full NeRF from scratch for each subject, as only a subset of parameters ($MLP_d$ and $\Phi$) are optimized. However, it is slower than purely feed-forward generalizable methods that require no optimization [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the pre-trained model weights. During fine-tuning, memory usage is similar to standard NeRF training but with fewer trainable parameters.
-- **Strengths:** Effectively reduces artifacts and imperfections for unseen subjects by allowing the model to adapt to specific high-frequency details and motion patterns [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Requires iterative optimization for each new subject, which is computationally more expensive than inference-only methods. Performance depends on the diversity and quality of the pre-training dataset [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Significantly faster than training from scratch because the search space is reduced by freezing $MLP_B$ and using pre-trained weights as initialization [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing gradients for only $\theta_d$ and $\theta_\Phi$, reducing memory footprint during the fine-tuning phase [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Effectively removes artifacts and recovers high-frequency details for unseen persons [S1]. Efficient adaptation to new identities [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial generalizable training; if the pre-trained model fails to capture the subject's basic structure, fine-tuning may not suffice [S1]. Performance depends on the sparsity of input views [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Fast per-subject fine-tuning was introduced in the context of HumanNeRF, an efficient method for generating human radiance fields from sparse inputs [S1]. The method addresses the challenge of transferring motion and appearance to unseen persons, where artifacts often arise due to limited training data and diversity among identities [S1]. It builds on the principle that certain components of the radiance field (like feature blending) can be learned generally, while others (deformation and specific radiance) need per-subject adaptation [S1].
+This method was introduced in the context of HumanNeRF, a framework for efficiently generating human radiance fields from sparse inputs [S1]. It addresses the limitation that generalizable models, while efficient, may produce artifacts for specific unseen individuals due to data diversity and sparsity [S1]. The approach is inspired by image-based rendering techniques that use adjacent views to refine textures [S1].
 
